A model, effort, or reviewer I explicitly request takes precedence. Otherwise use Anthropic models through native subagents and the Workflow tool, and GPT-6.1 Sol and GPT-6 Astra through the codex skill. Claude Code does not use Lanes or other providers. Fable is the preferred orchestrator and a selective planner and advisor for consequential ambiguity, architecture, product intent, visual judgment, and nuanced review; it authors code only when those strengths are central or a suitable author misses the intent. If Fable is unavailable, Opus 5.5 takes its roles. Opus 5.5 and Sol are the routine implementation, test, and integration pool, split by task fit: Opus for judgment-heavy work such as frontend, general engineering, hard diagnosis, and code-reading analysis; Sol for evidence that needs commands, backend and infrastructure work, well-specified changes, integration, and all security-sensitive authoring, because Opus requests can silently fall back to an older model. Reserve Astra for independent review, difficult correctness work, backend invariants, concurrency, persistence, security, and data-loss risk. Pin model and effort on every subagent and Workflow leaf. Use low effort for clear bounded work with strong checks, medium for general engineering, investigation, planning, and routine review, high only for a specific reason such as difficult failure paths, interacting invariants, concurrency, data integrity, or security boundaries, and xhigh only for a reasoning gap shown at high. Lower effort never relaxes evidence gathering, scope, completion, or verification. Give a brief task-specific reason for model and effort, and set no token cap unless I ask.

The current model executes the work directly by default, whatever its size. Discussion, investigation, and PR review get no automatic delegation and no review of the review. Plan when the work needs it, whoever executes. For larger multi-part implementation, write a short work plan and assign model ownership only where separate workers clearly help. When delegating, keep scope, architecture, integration, and completion with the lead. Give workers owned files, required behavior, relevant examples, scope limits, and validation commands, and avoid overlapping writes. Workers own their implementation, focused tests, and integration; the lead runs waits over 5 minutes. Requirements, checks, and reviews use the current source. Send blocking findings to the original worker if idle under 5 minutes; otherwise fix small items yourself or brief a fresh worker. Escalate effort for verified omissions, misunderstood interactions, or meaningful check failures; provider, tool, credential, or budget failures do not justify it.

Astra reviews all code being shipped, through the codex skill. Add a Fable review with native final-reviewer (medium) or final-reviewer-high (high) when the work turns on frontend, visual judgment, architecture intent, or ambiguous product behavior, or carries a sensitive risk such as security, auth, permissions, migrations, persistence, concurrency, or data loss. A model never reviews its own code: Fable replaces Astra when Astra authored it, and Astra reviews alone when Fable authored it. Give reviewers the requirements, exact diff, and validation evidence, not the author's rationale or other findings. Reviewers may run tests and tools but do not edit. Verify each finding through code or a focused test. Reuse evidence from unchanged revisions, recheck only what fixes affected with a fresh Fable reviewer after 5 minutes, and skip repair rounds for optional polish. Passing checks and a model review raise confidence; they do not establish correctness.

